# Ebb — Cold Start

State lives in git and the work graph, so this works after any shutdown, reboot, or fresh clone. The one thing written on the way out is the handoff note — see Shutdown below and `wheelhouse/runbooks/RUNNING_THE_LOOP.md`'s "End of day".

## Minimum viable fleet

Commander + one worker + one reviewer. Start there. It is a working loop, and it is what makes this installable rather than aspirational.

## 1. Commander

```bash
cd ~/code/ebb
claude
```

The folder's `CLAUDE.md` makes this session the commander. No launcher required.

**If `wheelhouse/HANDOFF.md` exists, read it before anything else** — it is
yesterday's commander writing to you: which seats to `resume` versus respawn,
what is waiting on a verdict, the first dispatch to make. After ingesting it,
archive it to `wheelhouse/handoffs/<sign-off date>.md` per the runbook's "Next
morning" section. No note is the normal first-morning state — start from the
graph.

> If you run your own agent harness, launch the commander with it instead — the folder context does the rest.

## 2. Seats

Each seat is a persistent `pi` process the commander owns — provisioned once, then started with the adapter. Nothing here is a terminal you keep open, and there is nothing to paste into a seat: the role brief is injected at spawn. `wheelhouse/fleet/SEATS.md` is the contract; `seats/README.md` documents every command below.

Provision each roster seat once (safe to re-run; it changes nothing for a seat that exists). The script creates the seat's agent directory under `$HOME/.pi-seats-ebb/`, pre-grants trust for the project root, and prints the one-time login for the account that seat should BE — run that login before the first spawn:

```bash
cd ~/code/ebb
seats/seat-env.sh ebb worker-1
seats/seat-env.sh ebb reviewer
```

Then, on any later cold start, just spawn from the roster:

```bash
bun seats/adapter.ts spawn worker-1
bun seats/adapter.ts spawn reviewer
bun seats/adapter.ts status          # every seat, liveness
```

`resume <seat>` instead of `spawn` reattaches a seat to the session it was stopped with, context warm — use it when a stop or reboot interrupted work in flight.

## 3. Watch it

The commander dispatches by name (`bun seats/adapter.ts dispatch <seat> <bead-id> ...`) and each seat's event stream lands in `seats/logs/<seat>.jsonl`. The bridge is the cockpit for watching the fleet: `seats/cockpit.sh ebb` builds (or re-attaches to — never duplicates, and re-runs respawn a missing floor pane) the tmux session `wh-ebb`, commander pane on the left and the floor — `bun seats/floor.ts`, read-only: spotlight one seat, rail of every seat's attention cues — on the right. Cockpit also owns the Dispatch Office herald: every launch verifies it, starts it when missing (`herald started: pid ...`), restarts it when dead (`herald dead: ...; restarting`), and later inbox traffic leaves `poke sent`, `poke cooldown`, or `poke not-idle` proof in `seats/logs/herald.out.log`. Keys and cue meanings are in `seats/README.md`'s "The bridge" section; `bun seats/adapter.ts status` is the no-cockpit fallback that always works, but it does not start the herald.

## 4. Feed it

Speak backlog items to the commander in plain language. It files them as beads, the fleet claims them, workers report done on the bead with comments and the `needs-review` label without closing, the reviewer gates them with bench evidence, and only the commander/integrator closes after merge per this project's merge policy.

## Shutdown

The end-of-day ritual, in order (`wheelhouse/runbooks/RUNNING_THE_LOOP.md`,
"End of day"): dispatch each active seat a write-your-handoff request — the
seat answers on its bead — then compose the fleet note at
`wheelhouse/HANDOFF.md` (per-seat status, waiting verdicts, tomorrow's first
actions), and only then:

```bash
bun seats/adapter.ts stop-all
```

Graceful, sessions kept; busy seats are reported and left running. A plain
machine shutdown loses nothing the graph holds — in-flight work resumes from
the graph, and `resume` brings a stopped seat back warm — but it skips the
handoff, and tomorrow's commander starts from raw state instead of a note.
